## 1. Introduction
Volatile organic compounds (VOCs) are the predominant pollutants of air pollution. Most VOCs cause damage to the ozone layer and form photochemical smog with other air pollutants, causing irreversible damage [1]. Due to the hazardous characteristics of VOCs, the emission of VOCs into the air environment has been strictly controlled in recent years. At present, the existing purification technology is mainly divided into two categories: adsorption and catalysis. However, in the process of adsorption, the adsorbent used easily caused secondary pollution in the treatment process. On the other hand, catalytic technologies including photocatalysis, thermal catalysis, plasma catalysis, and photothermal catalysis are excellent strategies for VOCs reduction. Photocatalysis is a new type of environmental protection and energy-saving technology with the advantages of easy operation, clean energy and no secondary pollution introduced, but the current photocatalysis technology is slow in efficiency and limited by the external environment [2–4]. Thermal catalysis is a conventional method that can reduce VOCs efficiently, but it consumes more energy [5,6]. Photothermal catalysis is a synergistic effect that can realize the full spectrum utilization of sunlight and solve the problem of high energy consumption, but photothermal catalysis requires high catalysts and has low conversion efficiency to solar energy [7,8]. Plasma technology has a fast response and can treat various VOCs at room temperature and pressure, but plasma catalysis has the problem that the reaction process may produce highly toxic intermediates [9,10]. At this stage, photocatalysis and thermocatalysis technologies are the main research directions, and photothermal catalysis is gradually becoming a new hot research topic due to its synergistic effect. Research on catalysis has also shifted from the removal of single pollutants to the exploration of the degradation mechanism and kinetics of multicomponent pollutants [11,12]. An important part of catalytic technology is the catalyst; therefore, the development of the preparation of low-cost, high-performance catalysts and an in-depth understanding of the catalytic reaction mechanism are essential for the development of catalytic degradation technology.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are inorganic–organic porous crystalline materials. The earliest application of MOFs was in gas storage/separation [13–15]. With the increasing attention of scholars from all circles and based on the advantages of high porosity and adaptability, MOFs have gradually been extended to many fields, such as wastewater treatment, gas catalysis and molecular sensing [16–21]. In the last few years, MOFs have been widely applied in the field of catalysis. MOFs have a high specific surface area and adjustable pore size; they have a large number of electron-deficient open metal sites, which can act as chemisorption sites to produce strong interactions with VOCs, and the adsorbed gases will be degraded in situ under light. These properties bring a broader prospect for the research of MOFs materials in pollutant oxidation [22,23]. To date, many researchers have extensively investigated the application of MOF-based materials in VOCs pollution control; among them, the application of multiphase catalysis is the most prominent, mainly including photocatalysis, thermal catalysis, photothermal catalysis, and plasma catalysis (Fig. 1).
Although there have been reviews on the application of MOFs in the environmental field, most of them focused on the removal of VOCs or the removal of specific pollutants in a single catalytic manner [24,25]. For example, He et al. summarized the study of MOFs, MOF derivatives and their composites in air pollution, such as VOCs, sulfides, and $\mathsf{N O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ [26]. Lu et al. discussed recent research progress on photocatalytic selective oxidation reactions involving MOFs according to different reaction types [27]. The review of
MOF materials by Xia et al. focused on the photocatalytic degradation of organic pollutants and introduced the practical applications of MOFs [28]. He et al. presented the recent progress of MOFderived polymetallic nanomaterials in the application of electrocatalysis and summarized the means of synthesis of MOFconstructed polymetallic nanomaterials [29]. The review reported by Sen et al. focused on the degradation of organic matter in water by MOFs and their derivatives while suggesting possible degradation mechanisms and providing an outlook on the future of the application of MOFs and their derivatives to wastewater treatment [30]. Combining the above views, the review of MOFs in the overall nonhomogeneous catalytic degradation of VOCs was very limited at this stage, and the review of MOFs and their derivatives mainly focuses on their application in wastewater treatment. The effects of different MOFs and their derivatives on the removal of VOCs lack in-depth elaboration. To fill this gap, our group carried out many studies. We studied the degradation of typical VOCs by different catalytic approaches, prepared many catalysts with excellent performance, and explored their catalytic mechanisms. Combined with the existing research results in the group, this paper presented a systematic review of the research on the catalytic degradation of various gaseous VOCs by MOFs and their derivatives and proposes a possible catalytic mechanism. First, the various types of multiphase catalysis were classified and summarized. The focus was on summarization of the field from different research directions, e.g., different VOCs and different types of catalysts. Possible photocatalytic oxidation mechanisms as well as catalytic mechanisms present in thermal catalysis are introduced. The current status of research and future prospects of MOF-based catalysts in this field are presented.
## 2. MOFs-based and MOF-derived materials for photocatalytic VOCs oxidation
Photochemical reactions are widely present in the atmosphere, while the presence of VOCs in the atmosphere could be hazardous to the surroundings and people’s health. Therefore, it is of practical significance to explore the photocatalytic degradation of VOCs for air pollution control. The photocatalytic oxidation reaction used semiconductors and light as catalysts and energy, respectively, to reduce organic matter into ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , which is an advanced technology for effective, reliable and eco-friendly purification. On the basis of the oxidation–reduction ability of photocatalysts under light conditions, MOFs and their derivatives are effective catalysts for pollutant degradation. Photocatalysis technology is also a promising research direction for the direct utilization of solar energy in renewable energy conversion and environmental pollution remediation. MOFs have been widely studied as catalysts for the degradation of VOCs. This section mainly introduces the application of MOFs in the photocatalytic treatment of VOCs. Among them, the recent studies of MOF-based and MOF-derived photocatalysts for the treatment of VOCs and their degradation effects are summarized in Table 1 and Table 2, respectively.
## 2.1. MOFs and MOF-based composites
## 2.1.1. Removal of aromatics
Aromatics are common VOCs. Aromatics emitted into the atmosphere mainly react with free radicals to form secondary organic aerosols and reduce the visibility of the atmosphere. Recently, a significant number of studies have been conducted on the photocatalytic degradation of aromatics, in which toluene and benzene were mostly reported as typical target pollutants. Therefore, efficient photocatalysts for the degradation of different aromatics are summarized in this section.
Toluene is often considered a model organic compound for gasphase photocatalytic oxidation, which is widely used and can form secondary aerosols in the atmosphere. Degradation of toluene is also one of the essential parts to combat atmospheric problems, and photocatalysis is an important means to degrade toluene. We synthesized a black phosphorus (BP)-Universitet i Oslo-66 (UiO-66) composite for the photocatalytic degradation of toluene by using an in situ preparation method (shown in Fig. 2). The typical two-dimensional (2D) material BP exhibited excellent photoresponse and anisotropic e-transport characteristics due to its distinctive physico-chemical and electronic structure. The strong bond formed between ${\cal Z}\mathrm{r}^{4+}$ and BP (Zr-P bond) enhanced the charge transfer from the $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ atom in UiO-66 to BP. The hydrophilic porous reticular UiO-66 encapsulation layer conferred enhanced visible light capture, charge transfer and photocatalytic activity to BP
UiO. In addition, BP-UiO exhibited enhanced redox/acidic properties and significantly improved the efficiency of toluene degradation [4]. The researchers found that different methods could be applied to strengthen the photocatalytic activity of the composites, such as structural changes of the composites, dopant modification, and construction of heterojunction structures. Zhang et al. synthesized nanocomposites with various properties, such as a large surface area, three-dimensional structure and excellent photoresponse, by using $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ in conjunction with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $75\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -25UN) [31]. The higher specific surface area enhanced the light absorption capacity, and the 3D porous structure could diffuse oxygen rapidly and form oxygen-rich conditions at the active site to enhance the activity. $75\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -25UN exhibited superior photocatalytic activity and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ selectivity over short-term residence times, and the toluene conversion rate reached $72.7\ \%$ Our group also performed much research to improve the properties of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and MOF composites. We prepared a new octahedral photocatalyst ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}–{\mathrm{MIL}}\ –101({\mathrm{Cr}})$ , which had type-II heterojunctions and surface heterojunctions, and improved the photocatalytic performance by enhancing the strong interfacial contact between the crystalline surface and MIL-101 and promoting the synergistic effect of effective charge transfer [32]. The latest experiments on titanium dioxide and MOF composites studied by our group pointed out that rutile anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ could form type-II heterojunctions by encapsulating MIL-101 to further construct heterojunctions and improve the utilization of photogenerated electrons [33]. The crystal structure of the material is shown in Fig. 3. The construction of heterogeneous junctions facilitated the splitting of optically generated electrons and inhibited the reorganization of electrons and holes. The combination of holes and oxygencontaining free radicals could achieve efficient degradation of toluene. The experiments showed that the conversion of toluene reached $91.4\%$ after $480~\mathrm{{min}}$ of reaction.
Benzene is a common solvent used in industry to produce other intermediate chemicals; for example, benzene can be used in a series of reactions to produce phenol for resins and adhesives. However, these types of conversions are more complicated to perform. Photocatalytic selective hydroxylation of benzene has been proposed and has led to a new wave of research [34]. Photocatalysts for the selective hydroxylation of benzene to phenol have been reported. However, the benzene conversion was low, the phenol selectivity was poor, and the performance was not satisfactory.
Therefore, it was essential to develop a new photocatalyst with excellent performance for the highly selective hydroxylation of benzene. In all reported MOF-based photocatalysts, the MOF of Fe was considered a suitable photocatalyst for phenol generation by benzene hydroxylation owing to the presence of iron-oxygen (Fe-O) clusters and easy activation of hydroxyl radicals. Wang et al. demonstrated that MIL-100 (Fe) and MIL-68 (Fe) could selectively hydroxylate benzene to produce phenol under visible light with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as an oxidant [35]. The production of benzene hydroxylation to phenol was an economic, sustainable and green process. The development of efficient photocatalysts for the selective oxidation of benzene to phenol is of great importance.
Chlorobenzene (CB) is a colorless and flammable aromatic compound used primarily as an intermediate in dyes, pharmaceuticals, pesticides, organic synthesis, and as a solvent. Some studies have shown that $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ can also degrade CB under light conditions [36,37]. Zhang et al. investigated the effect of humidity on the decomposition of gas-phase CB using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (anatase) in an intermittent photocatalytic reactor [38]. It was found that increasing the relative humidity led to an increasing adverse effect on the rate of adsorption and photocatalytic decomposition of CB. Based on the above theory, we synthesized titanium-based MOFs with properties such as strong redox, high visible light response and good water stability to achieve efficient degradation of chlorobenzene [39]. The synthesis process is shown in Fig. 4. It was shown that the synthesized catalyst could completely degrade chlorobenzene within a short period of light, while pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ could only degrade $35\%$ of chlorobenzene. The degradation rate of chlorobenzene was maintained after the introduction of water during the experiment, indicating that the material was resistant to water. This work demonstrated a strategy for more efficient degradation of chlorobenzene and suggested possible degradation pathways. Our group also investigated efficient photocatalysts for degrading other chlorinated VOCs. As a representative component of chlorinated VOCs (CVOCs), o-dichlorobenzene is of great concern for its carcinogenicity, mutagenicity and toxicity. We prepared a BP-UiO with enhanced visible light capture capability [4]. The photodegradation efficiency of BP-UiO for o-dichlorobenzene was verified by experiments up to $91.5\mathrm{~\%~}$ . In addition, we also investigated the effect of water on the catalyst. Even in the presence of water, BPUiO had good catalytic properties for o-dichlorobenzene. The stability of the material was also confirmed by cyclic degradation experiments. This report provided possible photodegradation pathways of o-dichlorobenzene by in situ Infrared Radiation (IR) spectroscopy and Gas Chromatography–Mass Spectrometer (GC– MS), and provided technical support for its application in the degradation of CVOCs.
## 2.1.2. Removal of OVOCs
Oxygenated VOCs (OVOCs), such as aldehydes (formaldehyde, acetaldehyde), alcohols (methanol and benzyl alcohol), ketones, ethers and phenols, mainly originate from the oxidation of emissions from motor vehicles and are generated during the use of solvents and emission of natural vegetation [40]. OVOCs could also come from the photochemical conversion of hydrocarbons and are intermediate products of atmospheric photochemical reactions [41]. This subsection focuses on a summary of photocatalytic applications for the degradation of aldehydes and alcohols.
A variety of catalysts have been developed and explored for the photocatalytic treatment of HCHO. Titanium, especially $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -based catalysts, is one of the most common catalysts for the treatment of HCHO. A number of materials, including silica and activated carbon, have already been employed to improve the overall catalytic performance of titanium-based nanostructures for HCHO degradation, but conventional materials have poor adsorption capacity for relatively low levels of HCHO [42,43]. Recent studies showed that the cross-aldehyde-alcohol condensation reaction of aminofunctionalized MOF had higher initial activity and could absorb more HCHO molecules [44]. A study by Huang et al. also confirmed that the aminated MOF had a larger specific surface area and smaller pore size with a stronger adsorption effect [45]. The ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}@{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ - MIL-125 photocatalyst for the UV-catalytic oxidation of HCHO was successfully prepared by an in situ solvothermal method. Due to the abundant $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathsf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ and $-0\mathsf{H}$ functional groups on the surface, specific microporous structure, suitable pore capacity and large surface area, the material had more adsorption sites. The catalyst showed better photocatalytic activity due to its tight interfacial contact and efficient interfacial charge transfer. This work provides a new idea for the exploitation of materials for air purification. Studies have proposed that isocyanate groups $(-\mathsf{N}{=}\mathsf{C}{=}0)$ have great potential in converting UV-responsive semiconductors such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ into visible light photocatalysts [46,47]. Catalysts containing highly reactive isocyanate groups $(-\mathsf{N}{=}\mathsf{C}{=}0)$ could enhance visible light absorption and promote electron transfer. Wang et al. synthesized porous zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 nanocrystals (ZIF-8-T) driven by visible light by means of a simple thermal treatment [48]. The formation of the isocyanate group $\scriptstyle(-\Nu=\complement=0)$ was the key to the excellent photocatalytic performance of ZIF-8-T. The specific surface area of the catalyst was increased, and the light absorption range was greatly expanded. Through coupled adsorption and photocatalytic oxidation, ZIF-8-T achieved efficient removal of HCHO under different light conditions.
Selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol to produce benzaldehyde is a green process that has emerged in recent years; it is environmentally friendly and has the potential to obtain high-quality benzaldehyde. Photocatalytic reactions are mild and green photochemical processes that meet the current requirements related to environmental protection. The development of efficient photocatalysts remains a major challenge for researchers. Many studies have confirmed that the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ linkage (Schiff base) is versatile in constructing extended $\pi$ -conjugated systems, and the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ linkage might improve the photocatalytic performance [49–51]. Wu et al. reported the synthesis of aromatic heterocyclic grafted catalysts $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) by a facile and novel postgrafting method [52]. The catalyst improved catalytic activity by forming a lower energy band gap through Schiff base chemistry between aldehyde and $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . This catalyst also demonstrated more than $85\%$ conversion and selectivity. Covalent organic frameworks (COFs) are two-dimensional (2D) and three-dimensional (3D) organic frameworks with large structures. Most COFs are connected by strong covalent bonds, and fewer COFs involve open metal sites [53,54]. Recently, researchers have worked to develop multiple methods to build multicomponent MOF/COF composites. Lu et al. successfully prepared efficient and stable $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL$125@$ TAPB-PDA hybrids based on the Schiff base reaction between aldehyde of monomer and $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathsf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ groups on MOFs [55]. The catalyst conversion in photocatalytic tests was up to $94.7~\%$ , which was approximately 2.5 times higher than that of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 and showed good stability performance. The novel photocatalysts reported provide a promising strategy for the synthesis of efficient functional materials for the photooxidation of aromatic alcohols. In addition to compounding other materials, postsynthetic modification (PSM) could also be used to improve the catalytic activity of MOFs. PSM has attracted much attention as a flexible and effective way to improve the structure and performance of MOFs. The PSM of MOFs could be performed in different ways, such as anchoring new functional groups and mixing metal ions. In the papers, modified materials displayed the highlighted catalytic performance [56,57]. Vahid Safarifard reported a postsynthetic exchange (PSE) approach and prepared a mixed metal-based MOF, denoted TMU22. TMU-22 $\scriptstyle(Z\ n/C_{0})$ was synthesized by a postsynthetic method and examined in the photocatalytic oxidation of aromatic alcohols, and the structure is shown in Fig. 5A [58]. The catalyst could oxidize $65\%$ within $180~\mathrm{{min}}$ , showing good photocatalytic performance. Hosseini et al. believed that TMU-22 $\scriptstyle(Z\ n/C o)$ as a photocatalyst could be effective and selective. Recently, they successfully synthesized $\mathrm{TMU-}22/\mathrm{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}0$ via a sonochemical method (Fig. 5B) [59]. TMU-22 $\slash\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ -Co showed better photocatalytic performance of benzyl alcohol oxidation to benzaldehyde under visible light. The improved photocatalytic oxidation was attributed to the synergistic effect between $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ Co and TMU22. This study proposed a more promising method for the synthesis of novel photocatalysts, which provides a reference for further research in the future.
## 2.1.3. Removal of other VOCs
VOCs are formed from natural and anthropogenic sources, such as industrial waste gas and automobile exhaust. In the atmosphere, a series of VOCs did not exist as individual components and were mostly in the form of mixed gases that affected the environment and human health. Therefore, it was necessary to develop and study photocatalysts that could degrade well under the conditions of mixed VOCs. Benzenes, or monoaromatic hydrocarbons (abbreviated as MACHs), are a general term for benzene and its derivatives, which are common pollutants emitted from human activities. Benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene and xylene (BTEX) are representative of benzene. BTEX compounds were found in low concentrations in indoor environments, such as homes, cars and other buildings, and could cause serious illness.
MIL-100(Fe) is an Fe(iii)-containing MOF consisting of Fe(iii) octahedral chains and benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid with high chemical stability, water stability and light responsiveness [60]. The result showed that MIL-100(Fe) had good performance in catalysis. Lee et al. proposed a strategy to prepare a recoverable filter (PMF) using a high specific surface area, small MIL-100 (Fe) particles as the catalyst coating and porous nickel foam as the substrate [61]. The performance of the PMF was tested by building a filtration system and testing the BTEX and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ concentration variation in the test bench. The average adsorption efficiency of the PMF was calculated to be $94.25\%$ , and the photocatalytic treatment efficiency was $75.95\ \%$ . This experiment provides a novel idea for the application of MOF materials in the field of air purification.
In a recent study on the degradation of mixed VOCs by MIL-100 (Fe), Chen et al. synthesized MOF/MOFs xerogels (MOX) isomorphic junctions with better photocatalytic activity and obtained lamellar pores by modulating the anion ratio in the metal precursors [62]. The layered pores provided a channel for the migration of benzene, toluene, p-xylene, m-xylene and styrene (BTXS) to the active acidic sites. The experimental results showed that the MIL100(Fe)MOF/MOX homodimer exhibited high photocatalytic activity for the degradation of oxyethylene and other BTXS. The successful synthesis of the catalyst provided a new method to regulate the creation of MOFs with layered pores, which would facilitate the progress of research on the photocatalytic removal of gas pollutants.
## 2.2. MOF-derived photocatalysts
## 2.2.1. Removal of aromatics
$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ has always received attention as a photocatalyst with unique properties, such as thermal/chemical stability, low cost, nontoxicity, and corrosion resistance [63]. However, the broad forbidden band of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the low response to visible light, and the rapid complexation of the generated photogenerated electrons with holes affected its catalytic activity. A series of modified Ti-based materials have been studied to improve the photocatalytic performance of the materials. Ti-MOF has been widely used due to its advantages of tunable pore size, structure and good charge separation ability. Among them, the materials of institut Lavoisier-125 (MIL-125) were more widely used in photocatalytic applications. Some experiments have shown that titanium dioxide can be obtained from MIL-125(Ti) and exhibits excellent properties [64]. A study in our group successfully prepared homogeneous anatase/rutile $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ derived from MIL-125(Ti). The better catalytic activity of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was verified by various characterization and photocatalytic tests. It was found that the electron-hole pair separation ability was improved by constructing an anatase/rutile heterojunction, which showed a fast and uniform photocurrent response [65]. According to previous experimental experience, composites composed of two or more components have better physical or chemical properties than materials composed of a single component. From this point of view, another feasible strategy was the construction of composites by combining $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with MOF. For the first time, Chandra et al. successfully encapsulated $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles (NPs) in ZIF-8 by an in situ binding method [66], which enhanced the visible light absorption and photocatalytic activity. In addition, many reports have shown that the derivatives obtained by pyrolysis of ZIF-8 exhibit better properties and higher charge separation efficiency [67–69]. Qin et al. prepared $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped graphitic carbonmodified $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ catalysts $\scriptstyle\left(\mathbf{G}\mathbf{C}-\mathbf{N}-\mathbf{TiO}_{2}\right)$ by pyrolyzing ZIF-8 using a hydrothermal method [70]. GC-N provided abundant pyridine N for the photocatalytic process, which boosts the photogeneration electron transfer, thus facilitating the production of reactive oxygen species and improving the photocatalytic activity. In addition, the activity of ${\mathsf{G C}}{\mathsf{-N}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ was similar to that of $\mathsf{P t/T i O}_{2}$ , and toluene was almost completely removed in each cycle of the degradation cycle experiment. The high stability, durability and conductivity of ${\mathsf{G C}}{\mathsf{-N}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ had an active role in the degradation of toluene. In addition to modified catalysts promoting the production of active oxygen, the structure of the catalyst was also adjusted to some extent to form defects or construct porous structures and create conditions for the production of active oxygen. Li et al. successfully constructed $_{\mathsf{C o/N}}$ co-doped vacant carbon skeletons $({\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{N C}})$ by in situ transformation of ZIF-67 by dopamine condensation [71]. ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{N C}}$ had a high specific surface area and was capable of receiving more light, and the hollow porous structure showed multiple light scattering, which improved the light utilization of ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{N C}}.$ Based on the above factors, when photons were irradiated on ${C o/N C}$ photogenerated ${\mathrm{e}}^{-}/{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ pairs could react with $0_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorbed on the photocatalyst surface to generate reactive oxygen species, thus increasing photocatalytic toluene activity.
Benzene is the simplest aromatic hydrocarbon and a class of carcinogen with relatively strong toxicity. Hence, there is an urgent need for an immediate solution for effective methods to remove benzene. A recent study showed that the degradation of benzene could reach $99.1\ \%$ and mineralization could reach $72.0\%$ by using mesoporous discoidal $\mathsf{N}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ as a photocatalyst. In this work, Zhao et al. prepared disc-shaped mesoporous anatase/rhodochrosite mixed-phase $\mathsf{N}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ by calcining a mixture of MIL-125 (Ti) and melamine [72]. It was shown that varying the annealing temperature during the synthesis process could yield catalysts with different $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -doping levels and phase structures, and an annealing temperature of $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ had the best catalytic performance. However, the high conversion and selectivity of benzene remain challenges. Sun et al. successfully designed and prepared carbon nitride (CN) with $\mathtt{C u O}$ thermally polymerized composites with high selectivity and conversion by calcination of Cu-MOF/TCN precursors [73]. The tight binding of CuO nanoparticles to CN endowed the material with superior electron trapping ability, promoted the shift of photogenerated electrons from CN to $\mathtt{C u O}$ and effectively inhibited the complexation of electrons and holes. It showed superior catalytic performance in the selective oxidation of benzene to phenol.
Xylene is a special class of benzene with three isomers (mxylene, o-xylene, and p-xylene) and is widely used in paints, preservatives, adhesives, varnishes, etc. It was shown that pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ had a small visible light response range and poor photocatalytic degradation of xylene, but efficient degradation of xylene could be achieved by using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and other transition metals or other dispersion methods [74–76]. Transition metals, particularly Cu, are often utilized to modify $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ because these metals promote interfacial electron transfer, while Cu narrows the band gap of the catalyst [77]. The distribution of metals or metal oxides on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface was crucial to the catalytic performance. MOF materials with highly ordered flexible topology and large specific surface area have been widely used as dispersion catalysts. Therefore, Zhou et al. proposed a novel approach to synthesize Cu-modified $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ by the combination of a blackening process using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ catalyst hydrogenation to generate large amounts of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ and Cu-MOF [78]. It was shown that the removal of xylene by the catalyst synthesized by the blackening process was 3.45 times higher than that prepared by the impregnation method under UV and visible light. $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ could also be compounded with other $n$ -type semiconductors to form photocatalysts with suitable energy band structures and good stability, while MOFs could be used as precursors to prepare photocatalytic materials with specific morphologies and porous structures. Guo et al. prepared disk-shaped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanosheets with mesoporous structures using MIL-125 as a precursor and synthesized $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -CdS/Ni bifunctional catalysts [79]. It was experimentally verified that $\mathfrak{p}$ -xylene (PX) could be oxidized to $\mathfrak{p}$ -methylbenzaldehyde (TALD) by the catalyst under light conditions, and the TALD obtained by oxidation could be widely used in pharmaceuticals, dyes, pesticides, polymers, etc.
## 2.2.2. Removal of OVOCs
MOF derivatives are potential photocatalyst candidates due to their excellent properties. MOF derivatives synthesized by MOFs as precursors or sacrificial templates have better stability and conductivity while retaining a large specific surface area and high porosity, among which UiO-66 is more frequently reported as a research target. Duan et al. presented a new study on the degradation of HCHO [80]. A nanoparticle structured composite $A g@Z\Gamma-$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}{\scriptstyle-1000\mathrm{U}6}$ based on UiO-66 with good dispersion and stability was prepared. The loading of Ag led to a wide range of light absorption of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and the doped zirconium led to a leftward shift of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ peak, reduced the band gap energy and increased the degradation efficiency. The HCHO degradation rate of the catalyst was $83.4\mathrm{~\%~}$ under $^\textrm{\scriptsize3h}$ of light. Recently, Duan et al. proposed that $\mathsf{A g@g{-}C_{3}N_{4}/Z r O_{2}{-}300}$ prepared by adding $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ and calcined UiO-66 has a highly dispersed structure and good photocatalytic activity [81]. After experiments showed that the degradation of
7 ppm HCHO by $\mathsf{A g@g{-}C_{3}N_{4}}/\mathsf{Z r O_{2}{-}}300$ was $81.3\%$ under 3 h of light, this work provides some reference value for the application of MOF and its derivatives in the field of formaldehyde degradation.
## 2.3. Photocatalytic mechanism
A considerable number of reports on the photocatalytic oxidation of VOCs have been published. Due to the different types of VOCs, the morphology and structure of catalysts and reaction conditions, it was difficult to give an exhaustive connection or a perfect reaction mechanism. For the treatment of different VOCs, a suitable mechanism could be inferred based on the intrinsic changes of the catalytic process. For example, the emergence and transformation of new substances in the catalytic process was studied. Based on previous theoretical studies, reactive oxygen species (ROS) were proposed to illustrate the mechanism of the photocatalytic degradation reaction of VOCs.
ROS play a crucial role in the photocatalytic selective oxidation process. A large number of experimental reports suggested that under light conditions, photogenerated electrons generated by photocatalysts could generate reactive oxygen species with different reactants, which promoted effective photogenerated electron transfer and electron-hole pair separation [82,83]. For example, $\mathsf{N}/\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{n}$ -modified MIL-125, prepared by Gao et al., could produce strongly oxidizing $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ and $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ substances, and ROS acted in the catalytic reaction of pollutants and the conversion of target pollutants to water and carbon dioxide (Fig. 6) [84]. The modified MIL125 showed higher ${\mathrm{CH}}_{3}{\mathrm{CHO}}$ $(98\ \%)$ photocatalytic degradation activity. The role played by ROS in the photocatalytic process was also investigated by our group. In our scavenger experiments, we found that the addition of TEMPO and BQ significantly reduced the degree of photocatalytic degradation, confirming the dominant role of $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ and OH radicals in the photocatalytic degradation of toluene and chlorobenzene [39]. Additionally, we found that the production of specific reactive oxygen species was closely related to the conduction band (CB) and valence band (VB) of the semiconductor. Through the introduction of the amino group, the hybridization energy level of the material was closer to the Fermi energy level, so the energy band of the material was lowered, electrons were more easily excited from VB to CB, and more reactive oxygen species were generated. ROS affected the compounding of photogenerated electrons and holes in the photocatalytic process, and the high separation rate of photogenerated electrons from holes meant that the catalyst was more conductive and had better photocatalytic activity. ROS improved the efficiency of the corresponding photocatalytic selective oxidation reaction and paved the way for efficient degradation.
## 3. MOF-based catalysts for catalytic VOCs oxidation
Thermal catalytic oxidation is also an effective method to control VOCs pollution in industrial production. Organic waste gases can be converted into ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and other nontoxic or low-toxic substances by the action of catalysts. In recent years, researchers have developed new catalytic materials that can efficiently catalyze and purify VOCs, including noble metal catalysts, nonnoble metal catalysts, and metal oxide catalysts. The researchers also improved the effect of catalysis by constructing the interface effect between different noble metals, regulating the interaction between the metal active components and the catalyst support, improving the effective metal loading and dispersion or designing the morphology of the catalyst support [85–88]. This section introduces the thermocatalytic application of MOFs for the degradation of VOCs. Some reported thermal catalysts for VOCs degradation and their properties are summarized in Table 3, the characteristics and hazards of typical VOCs mentioned in the text are also summarized in Table 4.
## 3.1. MOFs and MOF-supported noble metal catalysts
Among various catalysts, noble metal (Pd, Pt, Ag, Ru and Au) nanoparticle supported catalysts were carefully explored, and the noble metal supported MOFs were an optimal choice for VOCs destruction due to their superior activity. However, the poor reliability of MOFs hinders their application in industrial catalysis. Compared with most MOFs, UiO-66 possessed better physical and chemical stability because of the strong affinity between Zr and O atoms [89]. UiO-66 has been widely studied for its high specific surface area and stability. Silver is a very typical noble metal and is commonly applied in catalysis for the degradation of VOCs. Li et al. synthesized a series of Ag/UiO-66 catalysts with different silver loadings for styrene oxidation [90]. The catalyst with 1.86 wt% Ag loading exhibited high styrene conversion, selectivity and stability during the reaction. The conversion of styrene increased with increasing temperature. In one of our works, we synthesized Ag/UiO-66 catalysts and investigated the effect of different Ag loadings on the structure and catalytic performance of toluene oxidation [91]. The structure of the composite catalysts with 2 wt% Ag loading was not disrupted. However, when the amount of Ag was increased, the structure of UiO-66 collapsed. The best catalytic performance was achieved when the Ag loading was $10~\mathrm{wt\%}$ (10Ag-U). The present report provided a proven method for determining the appropriate amount of silver loading on catalysts.
We further investigated a series of studies on other noble metals (platinum group metals) loaded on UiO-66. Platinum group metals, especially platinum and palladium, are widely used in catalysis due to their good catalytic activity. We successfully prepared a highly dispersed Pd nanoparticle-loaded UiO-66 catalyst by ethylene glycol reduction (Pd-U-EG) and evaluated its catalytic performance by toluene degradation [92]. Pd-U-EG had highly dispersed Pd NPs with a high lattice oxygen content and could completely convert toluene at $200^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}.$ In a new work, we investigated the synergistic aspects of active Pd species, which were not systematically explored in previous reports [93]. In this work, we prepared a catalyst with the coexistence of isolated Pd atoms $\left(\mathsf{P d}_{1}\right)$ subnano Pd clusters $(\mathrm{Pd}_{\mathrm{c}})$ and Pd nanoparticles $(\mathrm{Pd}_{\mathrm{n}})$ by continuous atmosphere conditioning $(1\%\mathsf{C O},20\%0_{2},79\%\mathsf{H e})$ at low temperatures. Residual nitrogen in the solvent was found to enter the catalyst structure to coordinate with Pd atoms through continuous atmosphere conditioning. The presence of pyridine N led to the formation of $\mathrm{Pd}_{1}$ with N coordination, which eventually led to Pd catalysts with the coexistence of three active Pd species. The stable and efficient catalyst exhibited excellent catalytic performance and water resistance for toluene oxidation.
In addition, we also conducted experiments on UiO-66 loaded with Pt nanoparticles loaded for gaseous toluene catalysis [94]. In this study, a series of UiO-66 catalysts loaded with different amounts of Pt were prepared by the wet impregnation method, and their catalytic performance was experimentally investigated. The catalytic efficiency of $0.5\ \%$ Pt-UH was the highest, and the degradation rate reached $90\%$ when the temperature reached $249^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}.$ The changes in catalyst structure and properties as well as the reaction mechanism were investigated before and after the reaction, and it was found that there was a phase transition between Pt0 and $\mathsf{P t O}$ during the reaction, and the catalytic performance was improved after the reaction, which might follow the Pt0-PtO synergistic catalytic mechanism. Recently, Li et al. proposed a versatile route for the preparation of metal/UiO-66 (metal ${\bf\Pi}=\mathrm{Pt}{\bf\Pi}$ , Pd, Ru) with high dispersion by impregnation reduction [95]. It was experimentally verified that Pt/UiO-66 NPs almost completely degraded toluene and ethyl acetate and exhibited high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ yields. This also showed that the combination of highly dispersed inert metals and MOF was a promising means of synthesizing catalysts.
## 3.2. MOF derivatives
Due to their rich and ordered porous structure, MOFs are easy to modify and design as support substrates for other catalytic materials or as sacrificial templates/precursors for the preparation of various functional structures [96]. Improved catalytic performance of MOFs through the acquisition of better performing derivatives or the introduction of some additional synergistic measures. This section summarizes the thermal catalytic degradation of VOCs by MOF derivatives.
## 3.2.1. MOF-derived mono-metal oxide catalysts
MOF-derived metal oxides have made great progress in CO oxidation applications. In our study, we found that the derived metal oxides have good catalytic properties, stability and excellent lowtemperature reduction behavior [97–105]. Metal oxides are commonly used as low-cost catalysts for the catalytic oxidation of VOCs. Recently, the MOF precursor method has been developed for the preparation of porous materials derived from MOFs to obtain metal oxides under thermal treatment, which provides more options for obtaining porous metal oxides [105,106]. Metal oxides prepared by the MOF precursor method have many excellent properties, such as highly dispersed active centers and strong physicochemical properties [26,107]. The large number of MOFs with different constitutive metals provided the possibility for the preparation of multiple metal oxides $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ ).
$\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ had good oxygen storage capacity and abundant acidic sites, which contributed to the catalytic combustion process. However, pure ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ catalysts cannot exhibit ideal catalytic performance due to the limitations of their own structure and physicochemical properties. Chen et al. successfully synthesized pure ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ ( ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ -MOF) by in situ pyrolysis of the Ce-MOF precursor in air [108]. The results showed that the catalyst with a pyrolysis temperature of $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ had good catalytic performance. In the higher temperature range, ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ was able to reach $100\%$ conversion quickly in a narrower interval. The method of Ce-MOF pyrolysis to $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ solved the problem of broadening the temperature region due to the high purity of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ in the high temperature region. Chen et al. also pointed out that different pyrolysis temperatures lead to different specific surface area values. The high pyrolysis temperature resulted in better crystallization, fewer oxygen vacancies and surface oxygen species, and toluene combustion showed reduced catalytic activity. Therefore, the effect of the thermal decomposition process of Ce-MOF at different temperatures and the effect of temperature on the structure of decomposition products were systematically investigated by Zheng et al. [109]. The thermogravimetric (TG) and X-ray diffraction (XRD) results showed that the crystals basically decomposed into ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ at $400~^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ . In addition, ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ (400) had the highest $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ and $\mathsf{V}_{\mathsf{p}}$ and more oxygen vacancies. This meant that the calcination temperature was $400~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , and the obtained material was more suitable as a carrier and catalyst.
In addition to $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ has been widely studied because of its versatility, low cost and easy synthesis. Co-MOF was the main parent for the synthesis of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , and one of the most common means was the pyrolysis of ZIF-67 to obtain $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . Zhao et al. synthesized a series of hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ polyhedrons by pyrolysis of ZIF67 of different sizes and shapes and investigated the effect of different MOF precursors on the catalyst performance [110]. As the particle size decreased, the catalyst activity increased significantly. In particular, the hollow $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}–400$ polyhedron with a high ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}/$ ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ atomic ratio exhibited the best catalytic performance and excellent stability. However, the relationship between ZIF-67 as a sacrificial template for the preparation of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ catalysts and calcination temperature has not been reported. Chen et al. prepared ZIF-67-derived $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\mathsf{-X}$ catalysts by controlling the calcination temperature [111]. The calcination temperature had an effect on the shape, pore structure, specific surface area and crystallization of the catalyst and had a profound effect on the redox state of the catalyst surface. Specifically, with increasing calcination temperature, the catalyst showed a three-dimensional flocculent structure with channels, the specific surface area decreased, and the pore size increased.
In addition, our previous study showed that the porous $\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ catalyst prepared by Mn-MIL-100 has good performance for the oxidation of CO. Subsequently, we synthesized a series of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ using Mn-MIL-100, Mn-MOF-74 and Mn-BTC and investigated the effects of different precursors on the catalytic performance of the catalysts for the oxidation of toluene [107]. The results showed that Mn- MIL 100- Ar - O exhibited good catalytic activity in catalysts prepared from different Mn- MOF precursors due to the high $\mathsf{M n}^{3+}/\mathsf{M n}^{4+}$ molar ratio of the material and the large number of oxygen species. The study provided a workable solution for finding more efficient catalysts for toluene.
## 3.2.2. MOF-derived composite metal oxide catalysts
According to previous reports, a large number of MOF-derived metal oxide catalysts have been successfully used for the degradation of different VOCs, but the catalytic activity of single metal oxides still suffers from poor activity. For example, $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ alone has poor structural thermal stability, and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ has good stability but suffers from weak oxygen mobility [112,113]. Therefore, the development of multimetal mixed metal oxide (MMO) catalysts for catalytic combustion of VOCs is an effective strategy that might be able to compensate for the lack of catalytic performance of single metal oxides. In this regard, a series of studies by Jia et al. and colleagues showed that MOF-derived mixed metal oxides had better degradation performance for different VOCs than single metal oxides. Chen et al. prepared a series of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathsf{C r}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ based on MIL-101- Cr composites by means of a combination of preparation methods and evaluated their properties by toluene catalysis experiments [114]. The introduction of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ in $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ could improve the oxygen storage capacity of the material and promote oxygen migration, which resulted in more oxygen vacancies and defects. The strong interaction between the two metal oxides facilitated the oxidation of VOCs. In addition, the Jia group investigated the degradation of chlorobenzenes by $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ composite catalysts. Hu et al. synthesized $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanocomposites with porous structures by introducing Mn ions through an impregnation method [115]. Benefiting from the physicochemical properties, such as porous structure, uniform suspension, and abundant lattice defects, $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{~/~}$ $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}–4\mathrm{h}$ showed better catalytic activity for CB oxidation. Multiple cycles of catalytic reactions showed good stability of the catalyst, and experiments with the introduction of water showed that the material had good resistance to water.
According to the available reports, the stability and catalytic effect of metal oxides mixed with $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ were enhanced. The electron transfer between Cr and Mn species facilitated the formation of surface oxygen defects, which enhanced the catalytic activity [116]. Therefore, the introduction of different metal ions (Ce, Co, Cr, etc.) into Mn-based oxides might have different effects on the catalytic performance of VOCs combustion. Among them, $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}^{-}}\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ catalysts were studied due to their high oxygen storage capacity and improved dispersion of transition metal oxides [117]. However, the reported specific surface area of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}^{-}}\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ catalysts was still relatively low, which affected the catalytic effect of the catalysts. Sun et al. prepared $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}^{-}}\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ materials with a high specific surface area by pyrolysis of Ce/Mn-MOF-74 [118]. The incorporation of Ce increased the specific surface area of the catalyst to $125.6\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , and the pore volume increased significantly. Additionally, the catalysts exhibited excellent catalytic activity for toluene. In previous studies, the activity of Mn-Ce catalysts was superior to that of single metal catalysts. However, the effect of different calcination temperatures of the MOF pyrolysis process on the performance of the prepared transition metal catalysts has rarely been considered. Therefore, in one of our studies, $\mathtt{M n C e O_{x}}$ catalysts with different calcination temperatures were synthesized. The effect of catalysts obtained at different pyrolysis temperatures on the catalytic activity of toluene was investigated [119]. It was confirmed experimentally that different calcination temperatures affected the surface area and pore capacity of the catalyst, which in turn affected the surface metal chemical state and oxygen vacancy concentration, oxygen adsorption capacity and reduction capacity. With increasing calcination temperature, the specific surface area of 1Mn1Ce increased, the pore volume decreased, and the metal-to-metal interactions were enhanced. The catalysts with a calcination temperature of $300^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ showed a better catalytic effect for toluene with high $\mathrm{O_{ads}}/\mathrm{O_{latt}},$ $\mathrm{Mn^{3+}/M n^{4+}}$ and $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}/\mathsf{C e}^{4+}$ molar ratios, more surface oxygen vacancies and better lowtemperature reduction performance. We also investigated the role of $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ on the oxidation of toluene by rod-shaped $\mathtt{M n C e O_{x}}$ [120]. We prepared $\mathtt{M n C e O_{x}}$ samples with different molar ratios by pyrolysis of Mn/Ce-BTC for the toluene oxidation reaction. 3Mn2Ce showed better toluene oxidation capability and higher $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ yield. The role of water vapor in the reaction was investigated by introducing $5\mathrm{vol\%}$ and $10\mathrm{vol}\%$ water vapor due to the presence of water vapor in toluene emitted from industry. Although the temperature for the conversion of $90\%$ toluene by 3Mn2Ce was maintained at $236^{\circ}C$ , it was interesting to note that the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ yield increased, an indication that the introduction of water vapor promoted the mineralization of toluene. Toluene programmed temperature rise desorption (toluene-TPD) and toluene programmed temperature rise surface reaction (toluene-TPSR) experiments were carried out under different conditions to further confirm the promotion of toluene mineralization by water and that water could promote toluene activation. This study provided a good strategy for the preparation of catalysts for the removal of high moisture VOCs. In addition, Wang et al. synthesized mixed transition metal oxides $(\mathsf{C e C u O_{x}})$ by pyrolyzing a bimetallic MOF [121]. Because of its abundant oxygen vacancies and special 2D structure, the performance of the $\mathsf{C e C u O_{x}}$ catalyst was significantly improved, and it could efficiently degrade methanol, toluene, styrene and acetone. Experiments also show that ${\mathsf{C e C u O}}_{\mathrm{x}}$ with its two-dimensional form may also help improve stability. This report provides a new way to develop stabilization catalysts.
## 3.2.3. MOF-derived supported noble metal catalysts
Although the catalytic performance of metal oxides obtained from the pyrolysis of MOF was improved, the catalytic activity of metal oxides was still poor compared with noble metal catalysts. However, the high cost and difficulty in dispersing precious metal catalysts limits their application. Therefore, a small amount of noble metals loaded on metal oxides might be a good choice to reduce the cost of catalysts and simultaneously improve the catalytic activity. Therefore, small amounts of noble metals were used as active components to enhance the catalytic activity of MOF derivatives [122,123]. For example, Li et al. prepared polyhedral $\mathsf{C o O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ -supported Pt, which exhibited great toluene oxidation performance. The excellent catalytic oxidation performance of toluene was found to be associated with a high structural defect height and abundant reactive oxygen species on the surface [124]. However, the effect of reactive oxygen on catalysts loaded with other noble metals has not been reported. Therefore, our group prepared MOF-derived $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ -loaded Ag catalysts and investigated the effect of the active oxygen species on the catalysts [125]. The results showed that the Ag-loaded Ce-MOF was prepared by the one-pot solvothermal method with the best results for toluene removal. The structure is shown in Fig. 7. The materials had good surface reduction, more active species and oxygen vacancies and strong stability. This study provided a new solution for the preparation of highly efficient and actively loaded MOF catalysts. Recently, our new study found that $\mathsf{P d@Z r O_{2}}$ obtained by preparing in situ grown $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based metal organic framework (MOF) as a sacrificial template has better anti-chlorine effect [126]. The catalysts formed $\mathrm{Pd}_{1}$ -Cl species and abundant oxygen vacancies (VO) during the pyrolysis process with enhanced performance in degrading VOCs. In addition, the $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Zr}0_{2}$ catalyst exhibited better water resistance and stability. This work may provide new strategies for the design of highly chlorine-resistant catalysts to improve the treatment of VOCs.
MOF-derived oxides with high specific surface areas, porous structures and abundant oxygen vacancies are very suitable as ideal carriers for the dispersion and anchoring of noble metals. However, single metal oxides as supports usually have difficulty exhibiting satisfactory catalytic activity for the oxidation of VOCs [114]. In recent years, the synthesis of pyrolytic MOF precursors for the preparation of different mixed oxides has been greatly developed [127,128]. The obtained materials usually displayed strong catalytic performance. For example, Sun et al. reported good catalytic activity, stability and recyclability of a Ce-ZIF-67-derived Pt/CeCo catalyst for toluene degradation [129]. MOF-derived mixed metal oxides loaded with noble metals have also been investigated in one of our works [130]. We prepared Mn3Ce2- 300 carriers by pyrolysis of Mn/Ce-MOF and loaded Pd nanoparticles by the impregnation method. Water resistance experiments, cycling experiments and stability tests showed that the catalyst had good water resistance and stability. The $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ temperatureprogrammed reduction results showed that the catalyst had good low-temperature reduction ability and strong interactions between different species. In addition, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) results showed the excellent performance of $\mathsf{P d}/$ Mn3Ce2-300 and the ability to completely oxidize toluene at $190^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ .
## 3.3. Catalytic mechanism
The mechanism of VOCs oxidation by different types of catalysts was different, which was affected by the nature of the active center, the nature of the support and the catalytic reaction conditions. The common VOCs catalytic mechanisms mainly include the Mars-Van Krevelen (MVK) mechanism, Langmuir-Hinshelwood (LH) mechanism and Eley-Ridea (ER) mechanism [131–133]. (a) The MVK mechanism assumes that the reactant molecules can be alternately reduced and oxidized upon reaction with the oxygenrich portion of the catalyst. (b) The LH mechanism was based on the principle of adsorption and desorption. It was a multiphase catalytic reaction with the participation of dual active states. (c) The ER mechanism was different from the L-H mechanism; the ER mechanism only had monatomic states participating in the reaction process.
The L-H model is commonly used to explain the mechanism of VOCs oxidation catalyzed by noble metal supported catalysts. In previous studies, we prepared a series of catalysts with different Pd loadings to degrade toluene, and DFT calculations revealed that this experiment satisfied the LH mechanism [93]. The toluene adsorbed on $\mathrm{Pd}_{\mathsf{C}}$ and $\mathsf{P d}_{\mathsf{N}}$ reacted with the oxygen that was adsorbed and activated by $\mathrm{Pd}_{1}$ . Toluene was mineralized by activated oxygen $(\mathbf{O}_{(\mathbf{ads})}^{-})$ and converted to ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ . In other words, these Pd species had synergistic effects on toluene degradation. In a recent study, we introduced gaseous $0_{2}$ substances into TPD and found a decrease in toluene desorption [134]. Calculation of the toluene degradation rate by toluene-TPD and toluene-TPSR confirmed the reaction between toluene and absorbed oxygen substances, and it could be inferred that the loaded Pd catalyst degradation of toluene follows the L-H mechanism. The catalytic mechanism of toluene oxidation over the loaded Pd catalyst is shown in Fig. 8. A study by Lei et al. showed that surface adsorbed oxygen rather than lattice oxygen played an important role in toluene combustion [135]. This verified that the catalytic oxidation of toluene on $\mathsf{M}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C o}_{1}\mathsf{C u}_{1}\mathsf{O}_{\mathsf{X}}$ follows the LH mechanism.
The MVK model is commonly used to explain the mechanism of VOCs oxidation over metal oxide catalysts. Several studies in our group have also confirmed this idea [120,130]. We investigated the oxidation process of toluene degradation by MOF-derived manganese cerium complex oxides. First, toluene was adsorbed and reacted with lattice oxygen and oxygen vacancies of the catalyst to convert to ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Then, the oxygen vacancies were supplemented by gaseous oxygen or lattice oxygen. In addition, we found that when $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ entered the reaction system, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorbed on oxygen vacancies in the form of associative adsorption to generate more active groups or provide more active sites with surface oxygen, which promoted the conversion of adsorbed oxygen to lattice oxygen and accelerated the mineralization of toluene. These reports also confirmed that the degradation of toluene by metal oxides follows the MVK model.
At present, research on catalytic mechanisms has mainly focused on noble metal catalysts and metal oxide catalysts, and studies on the structure of MOF derivatives and MOFs are limited.
The pollutants studied were single, and research on multicomponent and multi-influencing factors was limited. These are also urgent problems for future research to develop more potential catalysts.
## 4. Emerging applications of MOF-based catalysts for VOCs removal
Photocatalytic and thermocatalytic technologies were effective in converting VOCs to harmless gases, but the high energy demand of catalysts and the low light energy utilization of photocatalysts affected their usage [133,136]. In recent years, photothermal cocatalysis and plasma catalysis have attracted much attention because of their higher performance than photocatalytic and thermocatalytic processes alone.
To reduce excessive energy consumption during thermal catalysis and accelerate the photocatalytic reaction rate, a new composite catalytic method of photothermal synergistic catalytic oxidation was studied. Photothermal catalysis, as an emerging pollutant treatment technology, could effectively combine thermal catalysis and photocatalysis to expedite the removal of VOCs and the reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ [137,138]. In previous studies, MOF-derived metal oxides were reported to be an effective catalyst for the degradation of VOCs, but their catalytic performance under the conditions of light-driven thermal catalysis has not been investigated. Therefore, Chen et al. constructed a series of $\mathsf{N i O}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathsf{C o}_{3}0_{4}$ composites derived from a ZIF-67 template and explored their performance in the field of toluene removal under photothermal conditions [128]. The catalyst with a ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{N i}}$ molar ratio of 3:5 showed the best catalytic activity and durability, and the catalyst converted approximately $95\ \%$ of toluene and mineralized approximately $80\%$ , which was attributed to the strong interaction between the materials, specific internal structure, more sites of action and the synergistic effect of good redox properties. Furthermore, $\mathsf{N i O}_{\mathrm{x}}/$ $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ composites were able to absorb sunlight in the full spectral range and had a high utilization of light energy. Therefore, it would provide an effective way to harvest sunlight in VOCs control through the photothermal effect.
Bahri et al. investigated the ability of MIL-101, MIL-53, and CPM-5 to remove isobutanol and toluene in a dielectric barrier discharge (DBD) plasma-catalyzed reactor [142]. The catalysts had the advantage of more active sites, high oxidation capacity and fewer byproducts in the NTP system, and all of them were able to completely degrade isobutanol in a short experimental time. Toluene removal by MIL-101, MIL-53 and crystalline porous material (CPM-5) also all reached more than $90\%$ . This work demonstrated the potential of MOFs as catalysts for the degradation of VOCs in NTP systems, but the synergistic effect of MOF and NTP systems was not investigated in detail. In contrast, Huang et al. prepared various Mn-MIL-100 materials with split porous structures by adjusting the molar ratio of lauric acid (LA)/carboxylate ligands and systematically investigated the synergistic interaction between MOF and NTP systems [143]. It was found that the combination of catalyst and NTP system could significantly improve the removal efficiency of toluene and the conversion rate of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , changed the toluene decomposition pathway, and further suppressed $\mathsf{N O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ and $\phantom{+}0_{3}$ production. The modified MOF exhibited higher performance than the pristine MOF in toluene mineralization due to the formation of a layered porous structure. MOFderived metal oxides also exhibited higher performance than the original MOFs. Feng et al. synthesized the porous material $\mathsf{M n C o N i O_{x}}$ (MCNO) by pyrolyzing the precursors of polymetallic MOF-74 (Mn-Co-Ni) [144]. It was found that the mixed oxides exhibited higher toluene decomposition effects due to their larger specific surface area and good reduction properties. The combination of MOF-derived polymetallic oxides with NTP proposed in this study improved the catalytic effect of the material and opened a new path for the efficient degradation of VOCs.
Recently, the combination of nanoparticles and MOFs has received much attention for their superior performance, and many projects have been conducted for multiphase catalysis and gas storage in nanoparticle/MOF composites. Among them, Li et al. synthesized $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ metal nanoparticles (MNPs) with a stable prismatic dodecahedral structure retaining ZIF-8 [145]. The process of material synthesis is demonstrated in Fig. 9. In the experiments tested for catalyst activity, ZIF-8 loaded with $10\mathrm{\mg\TiO_{2}}$ had the best toluene conversion. In addition, the catalyst still had stable water resistance and efficient catalytic activity at $92.5~\%$ relative humidity. This study broadened the application of modified MOF materials in NTP catalysis, which could effectively and simultaneously control VOCs pollution.
## 5. Conclusion and prospects
To date, extensive research and breakthroughs have been made in the application of MOFs in VOC pollution control. With the aim of improving the catalytic activity of MOFs, researchers have explored different types of improvements, including loads of noble metals, functional modifications and composites of other materials. MOFs and MOF derivatives are more promising catalysts for the treatment of VOCs, which can provide highly adsorbed, stable porous structures with many active sites for catalytic reactions. Reviews related to MOFs and their derivatives have mostly focused on the application of the materials in the treatment of organic wastewater, and few reports have addressed MOFs and their derivatives for the degradation of VOCs [30,106,146,147]. The aim of this paper was to provide a comprehensive review of the catalytic applications of MOFs and MOF-derived materials in VOCs pollution control in recent years. Among them, MOFs and their derivatives are widely used in photocatalysis and thermocatalysis. In the field of photocatalysis, MIL-125 is mostly used as the base material for compounding or pyrolysis to prepare catalysts with better performance, while in thermal catalysis, MOF-derived metal oxides or precious metal-loaded MOFs and their derivatives are mostly used as the main catalytic systems, among which Co, Ce, Mn metal oxides and UiO-66 loaded with precious metals have better performance. In addition, based on the corresponding studies, the catalytic mechanisms for the removal of VOCs were discussed and summarized and provided a theoretical basis for the development of more efficient catalysts.
Although current studies have proven that VOCs can be removed through heterogeneous catalysis, there are still many problems that need to be solved to promote their practical application. (1) The current research mainly focused on the decomposition of single model VOCs in the catalytic system. In actual exhaust gas, VOCs exist in the form of mixtures. However, at present, there are few reports on the degradation of mixed VOCs, and the understanding of this system is not deep enough. (2) Due to their high cost and low synthesis efficiency, MOFs limit their large-scale application. (3) Catalytic degradation of VOCs showed excellent performance in laboratory-scale reactors, but its effect in actual production was not tested enough. Future research should provide an in-depth understanding of the catalytic reaction mechanism at the electronic, molecular and atomic levels to develop efficient and stable catalysts and preparation processes more precisely. Based on the above literature reports, MOF derivatives have more applications in the direction of thermal catalysis and fewer relevant reports in the field of photocatalysis, which should pay more attention to the application of MOF derivatives in photocatalysis, especially in the removal of VOCs. Future developments should also focus on the treatment of mixed VOCs, developing novel catalytic reactors and linking them to practical large-scale industrial applications.
